        <dc:subject>lens: Information-theory</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Token clustering/collapse theorey</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Bridging the Dimensional Chasm: Uncover Layer-wise Dimensional Reduction in Transformers through Token Correlation</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The geometric evolution of token representations in large language models (LLMs) presents a fundamental paradox: while human language inherently organizes semantic information in low-dimensional spaces ($\sim 10^1$ dimensions), modern LLMs employ high-dimensional embeddings ($\sim 10^3$ dimensions) processed through Transformer architectures. To resolve this paradox, this work bridges this conceptual gap by developing a geometric framework that tracks token dynamics across Transformers layers. Through layer-wise analysis of intrinsic dimensions across multiple architectures, we reveal an expansion-contraction pattern where tokens diffuse to a &quot;working space&quot; and then progressively project onto lower-dimensional submanifolds. Our finding implies a negative correlation between the working space dimension and parameter-sensitive performance of the LLMs, and indicates that effective models tend to compress tokens into approximately 10-dimensional submanifolds, closely resembling human semantic spaces. This work not only advances LLM interpretability by reframing Transformers layers as projectors that mediate between high-dimensional computation and low-dimensional semantics, but also provides practical tools for model diagnostics that do not rely on task-specific evaluations.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025/03/28</dc:date>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.22547v1</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2026-05-20 02:01:49</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:shortTitle>Bridging the Dimensional Chasm</z:shortTitle>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
    </bib:Document>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_166">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-citation-items=&quot;%5B%7B%22uris%22%3A%5B%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F20605311%2Fitems%2FEFMLNBK2%22%5D%2C%22itemData%22%3A%7B%22id%22%3A%22http%3A%2F%2Fzotero.org%2Fusers%2F20605311%2Fitems%2FEFMLNBK2%22%2C%22type%22%3A%22webpage%22%2C%22abstract%22%3A%22The%20geometric%20evolution%20of%20token%20representations%20in%20large%20language%20models%20(LLMs)%20presents%20a%20fundamental%20paradox%3A%20while%20human%20language%20inherently%20organizes%20semantic%20information%20in%20low-dimensional%20spaces%20(%24%5C%5Csim%2010%5E1%24%20dimensions)%2C%20modern%20LLMs%20employ%20high-dimensional%20embeddings%20(%24%5C%5Csim%2010%5E3%24%20dimensions)%20processed%20through%20Transformer%20architectures.%20To%20resolve%20this%20paradox%2C%20this%20work%20bridges%20this%20conceptual%20gap%20by%20developing%20a%20geometric%20framework%20that%20tracks%20token%20dynamics%20across%20Transformers%20layers.%20Through%20layer-wise%20analysis%20of%20intrinsic%20dimensions%20across%20multiple%20architectures%2C%20we%20reveal%20an%20expansion-contraction%20pattern%20where%20tokens%20diffuse%20to%20a%20%5C%22working%20space%5C%22%20and%20then%20progressively%20project%20onto%20lower-dimensional%20submanifolds.%20Our%20finding%20implies%20a%20negative%20correlation%20between%20the%20working%20space%20dimension%20and%20parameter-sensitive%20performance%20of%20the%20LLMs%2C%20and%20indicates%20that%20effective%20models%20tend%20to%20compress%20tokens%20into%20approximately%2010-dimensional%20submanifolds%2C%20closely%20resembling%20human%20semantic%20spaces.%20This%20work%20not%20only%20advances%20LLM%20interpretability%20by%20reframing%20Transformers%20layers%20as%20projectors%20that%20mediate%20between%20high-dimensional%20computation%20and%20low-dimensional%20semantics%2C%20but%20also%20provides%20practical%20tools%20for%20model%20diagnostics%20that%20do%20not%20rely%20on%20task-specific%20evaluations.%22%2C%22container-title%22%3A%22arXiv.org%22%2C%22language%22%3A%22en%22%2C%22title%22%3A%22Bridging%20the%20Dimensional%20Chasm%3A%20Uncover%20Layer-wise%20Dimensional%20Reduction%20in%20Transformers%20through%20Token%20Correlation%22%2C%22title-short%22%3A%22Bridging%20the%20Dimensional%20Chasm%22%2C%22URL%22%3A%22https%3A%2F%2Farxiv.org%2Fabs%2F2503.22547v1%22%2C%22author%22%3A%5B%7B%22family%22%3A%22Song%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Zhuo-Yang%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Li%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Zeyu%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Cao%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Qing-Hong%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Luo%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Ming-xing%22%7D%2C%7B%22family%22%3A%22Zhu%22%2C%22given%22%3A%22Hua%20Xing%22%7D%5D%2C%22accessed%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222026%22%2C5%2C20%5D%5D%7D%2C%22issued%22%3A%7B%22date-parts%22%3A%5B%5B%222025%22%2C3%2C28%5D%5D%7D%7D%7D%5D&quot; data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;While transformers employ high dimensional embedding, effective model tend to compress tokens into approximately 10-dimensional submanifolds, closely resembling human semantic spaces. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This work not only advances LLM interpretability by reframing Transformers layers as projectors that mediate between high-dimensional computation and low-dimensional semantics, but also provides practical tools for model diagnostics that do not rely on task-specific evaluations” &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies heavily on geometric intrinsic dimension metrics for diagnostics; exact applicability to significantly different or newer non-Transformer architectures isn't established.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
